    "fullContent": "# Automotive Module Repair and Programming in Iloilo\n\nModern vehicles use multiple electronic controllers, and a problem in one system can affect several functions. If you have been advised to replace or program a module, contact Autohex ECU Tuning & Remapping with the exact vehicle and controller details. Availability depends on the module, software and required procedure. Confirm support before buying a replacement, removing parts or booking transport to the workshop.\n\nModule enquiries may involve body electronics, transmission-related controllers or other supported systems. Do not assume one shop can repair every controller on every vehicle. A useful first message identifies the affected function, any dashboard messages and the evidence behind the recommendation. This makes it easier to discuss whether diagnosis, repair or configuration is the next step.\n\n## Identify the function that has failed\n\nDescribe what the vehicle does instead of relying only on abbreviations. For example, explain whether door functions are intermittent, a display is behaving incorrectly or a warning appeared after a component replacement. Include the sequence of events and any other systems affected at the same time.\n\nBegin with [automotive diagnostics](/services/automotive-diagnostics/) if the failed component has not been established. A communication code or inactive function does not prove that the controller itself is damaged. Power, wiring and configuration may need checking. Ask what evidence is needed before deciding that a module should be removed or replaced.\n\n## Repair, coding and programming are different\n\nRepair addresses a fault in a component where a suitable repair is possible. Coding or configuration sets supported options or vehicle-specific information. Programming writes software through an appropriate process. The terms are sometimes used loosely in enquiries, so ask which operation is actually proposed for your case.\n\nThe required work may involve more than one stage. A replacement controller can need compatibility checks and setup before the vehicle operates correctly. Ask whether the quoted scope includes the necessary configuration and verification. If the problem concerns the engine computer specifically, the [ECU repair page](/services/ecu-repair/) provides a more focused starting point.\n\n## Confirm replacement compatibility\n\nPhysical appearance is not enough to establish a match. Part numbers, hardware versions, software and vehicle equipment can matter. Send a safely obtained label photograph and the seller’s details before purchasing a secondhand module. Avoid accepting “same model” as the only compatibility check.\n\nTell Autohex whether the original module still communicates and whether any earlier workshop has attempted programming. Previous work can affect what information remains available. Confirm whether the complete vehicle, original unit and replacement are needed together. Do not post a module to the business without receiving instructions about acceptance and the appropriate assessment process.\n\n## Security and safety systems need the right process\n\nSome controllers interact with the immobilizer or other protected functions. Legitimate security work may require proof of ownership and authorization. The [IMMO and DTC page](/services/immo-dtc-solutions/) explains how to prepare that enquiry. Ask which documents and keys should accompany the vehicle.\n\nFor airbags, braking and other safety-related systems, describe the concern accurately and confirm the available specialist procedure. A reset alone does not establish that a safety system has been restored. Where collision damage or deployed restraints are involved, the complete repair requirements must be considered. Do not treat a cleared warning as proof that all necessary work is finished.\n\n## Plan the appointment and budget\n\nAsk how the initial assessment is charged and when a firm scope can be established. Parts availability, software access and the condition of the original controller can affect the process. Confirm how additional work will be approved, what documentation will be supplied and whether any warranty applies to the agreed job.\n\nDrivers from [Santa Barbara](/locations/santa-barbara/) and [Pavia](/locations/pavia/) should request the current workshop pin and receiving arrangements before travelling. If the vehicle has become immobilized after a replacement attempt, say so at the start. Do not assume on-site programming or emergency attendance is available.\n\n## Module programming questions\n\n### Can a used module be fitted without setup?\nSometimes a vehicle requires coding, programming or matching, and some replacements may be unsuitable. Confirm the exact part and proposed process before purchasing. Compatibility cannot be established from the connector shape alone.\n\n### Will programming repair damaged hardware?\nNot necessarily. Software work and physical repair address different issues. Ask what evidence suggests the fault is in the software or configuration and whether hardware checks are required first.\n\n### Do I need to bring the entire vehicle?\nThat depends on the job. Some assessments need the surrounding vehicle systems and original controller. Get instructions before removing components or arranging delivery.\n\n### How do I request an assessment?\nMessage the [Autohex Facebook page](https://www.facebook.com/AutohexEcuTuningRemapping) with the year, model, engine, affected function and any part numbers or reports. Include previous replacement or programming attempts and ask which service is supported.",
